# Apupiwe grammatical features mapped to Grambank v1.0.3 / WALS (CLDF v2020.6).
# Pipe-separated. Lines starting with # are comments.
# measures: DB:EXPR ; DB:EXPR ...  EXPR = PARAM=VALUES[&PARAM=VALUES...], VALUES comma-separated.
#   The first measure is primary (it drives class=auto). GB = Grambank (values 0/1/2/3), WALS = WALS code number.
# match: good (same category) / fair (close, some mismatch noted) / poor (only partly the same thing) / none (no database feature).
# class: auto (from the primary measure; thresholds in compute.py) or one of converged/common/regional/invented (judged, reason in note).
# tag (for non-database statements): sourced / inferred / recalled, per research-rigor.
id|feature|rule|apupiwe|measures|match|class|note
S01|Five-vowel system|r-2-1|a e i o u, no length or nasal vowels|WALS:2A=2|good|auto|WALS bins 5-6 qualities together.
S02|Small consonant inventory|r-2-2|13 consonants|WALS:1A=1|good|auto|WALS "small" = 6-14; "average" = 22 +/- 3. Apupiwe sits below the world mode.
S03|Voicing contrast in plosives only|r-2-2|p t k vs b d g; f s with no voiced partner|WALS:4A=2|good|auto|
S04|Full /p t k b d g/ set|r-2-2|all six present|WALS:5A=2|good|auto|
S05|Laryngeal contrast realised by both aspiration and voicing|r-2-2|reference p t k aspirated word-initially and b d g fully voiced, so either cue separates them|none|none|regional|No database codes the phonetic realisation. recalled, unverified: languages whose two-way contrast is aspirated vs prevoiced (Swedish, Norwegian; Helgason & Ringen 2008) are a small, mainly Germanic set. Apupiwe's double cue is a deliberate overspecification; the two-way contrast itself is S03.
S06|No uvulars|r-2-2|none|WALS:6A=1|good|auto|
S07|No glottalised consonants|r-2-2|none|WALS:7A=1|good|auto|
S08|One lateral /l/, no lateral obstruents|r-2-2|l|WALS:8A=2|good|auto|
S09|No velar nasal phoneme|r-2-2;r-11-2|[ŋ] only as variant; ng -> n in loans|WALS:9A=3|good|auto|
S10|No phonemic nasal vowels|r-2-9|vowel+n may sound nasal, not contrastive|WALS:10A=2|good|auto|WALS 10A sample is small (244).
S11|No front rounded vowels|r-2-1|none|WALS:11A=1|good|auto|
S12|No uncommon consonants (clicks, labial-velars, pharyngeals, th)|r-2-2|none|WALS:19A=1|good|auto|
S13|Syllable (C)V(n), onsetless only word-initially|r-2-3|one coda consonant, n only|WALS:12A=2 ; WALS:12A=1,2|good|auto|WALS: "Languages which permit a single consonant after the vowel ... are counted as having moderately complex syllable structure" (Maddieson 2013, WALS ch. 12, read in the CLDF chapter text). Apupiwe is at the simple end of that bin (one coda, n only). The word-initial-only onsetless rule has no database feature.
S14|Fixed initial stress, weight-insensitive|r-2-4|first syllable of every content word|WALS:14A=2 ; WALS:15A=8|good|auto|
S15|No rhythmic (secondary) stress|r-2-4;r-2-6|one stress per word, stepping down after it|WALS:17A=5|fair|auto|WALS 17A codes languages with rhythmic alternation; Apupiwe has none.
S16|No tone|r-2-8|pitch only for intonation and emotion|WALS:13A=1|good|auto|
S17|Declination of pitch across a phrase|r-2-7|each stressed syllable a little lower; reset after a pause|none|none|converged|sourced: "Because this pattern is attested in a great number of diverse languages, it has been proposed as a universal feature of intonation" (Gallaiford 1983, MA thesis, Univ. of Victoria); "It has been observed in many languages" (Yuan & Liberman, Speech Communication, ~2015). No world count.
S18|Interjections in shapes outside the phonology|r-2-10|bare vowel, two vowels, final y, no vowel|none|none|common|sourced: primary interjections "tend to be composed of a set of elements that is (i) smaller than and (ii) only partly overlapping with the larger phonological system"; "some degree of phonological or phonotactic deviance is common, it is not definitional" (Dingemanse, "Interjections", Oxford Handbook of Word Classes, preprint). Apupiwe makes the deviance a rule, which goes beyond the tendency.
S19|"Huh?" repair interjection|r-2-10|a?|none|none|converged|sourced: Dingemanse, Torreira & Enfield 2013 (PLOS ONE 8(11) e78273): "a universal word likely to be attested in similar form in all natural spoken languages" (10 languages compared, literature on 31).
S20|Lexicon kept clear of accent mergers and digit-like onsets|r-2-11;r-2-12|no two words differ only by a harmless-variant pair; no content word starts like a digit|none|none|invented|A design constraint on the word list; natural lexicons are not built this way (inferred). Not a typological feature as such.
M01|No inflection at all (isolating)|r-2-5|words never change form|GB:GB070=0&GB071=0&GB044=0&GB079=0&GB080=0&GB089=0&GB090=0&GB091=0&GB092=0&GB093=0&GB094=0 ; WALS:26A=1|fair|auto|Primary: the Grambank conjunction (no case, no plural affix, no other verb prefixes or suffixes, no person indexing), the closest match to "words never change form". WALS 26A "little affixation" is a looser bin and comes out common. Many related rows (no causative affix V39, no case M02, no person marking M03) follow from this one.
M02|No case|r-2-5|role by position and relation words|GB:GB070=0&GB071=0&GB072=0&GB073=0 ; WALS:49A=1|good|auto|
M03|No person marking on the verb|r-2-5|none|GB:GB089=0&GB090=0&GB091=0&GB092=0&GB093=0&GB094=0 ; WALS:102A=1|good|auto|
M04|No grammatical gender or noun classes|r-2-5;r-5-2-2|sex said by a modifier when it matters|GB:GB051=0&GB052=0&GB053=0&GB054=0&GB192=0 ; WALS:30A=1|good|auto|
M05|No gender in pronouns|r-10-3-1|mon "he/she"|GB:GB030=0 ; WALS:44A=6|good|auto|
W01|Subject-verb-object order|r-1-2|SVO|WALS:81A=2 ; GB:GB132=1|good|auto|GB132 = verb-medial (SVO or OVS).
W02|Fixed order of core arguments|r-1-2|words never move to show role|GB:GB136=1|good|auto|
W03|Verb-object-oblique (VOX)|r-4-1-2|verb, objects, then relation phrases|WALS:84A=1|good|auto|
W04|Prepositions|r-4-4-1|no, len, don ... before their noun|GB:GB074=1&GB075=0 ; WALS:85A=2|good|auto|
W05|Noun-adjective|r-5-2-1|guyi tugu|GB:GB193=2 ; WALS:87A=2|good|auto|
W06|Noun-demonstrative|r-5-2-8|guyi yin|GB:GB025=2 ; WALS:88A=2|good|auto|
W07|Noun-numeral|r-5-1-1|dili oma|GB:GB024=2 ; WALS:89A=2|good|auto|
W08|Noun-genitive|r-5-2-9|guyi pi fo mara|GB:GB065=2 ; WALS:86A=2|good|auto|
W09|Noun-relative clause (postnominal only)|r-5-3-1|ewo gi tedo|GB:GB327=1&GB328=0 ; WALS:90A=1|good|auto|
W10|Degree word before adjective|r-6-2-6|ke tugu, ten upe|WALS:91A=1|good|auto|
W11|'All' after the noun|r-5-2-8|... we|GB:GB203=2|good|auto|
W12|Adverbial subordinator clause-initial|r-4-3-3|sin, pu, kon, ya, bo + clause|WALS:94A=1|good|auto|
W13|Complementiser before its clause|r-4-7-5;r-4-8-5|me + clause|GB:GB421=1|good|auto|
W14|The whole head-initial bundle at once|r-1-2;r-1-4;r-5-1-1|SVO, prepositions, N-Adj, N-Dem, N-Num, N-Gen, N-Rel together|GB:GB132=1&GB074=1&GB075=0&GB193=2&GB025=2&GB024=2&GB065=2&GB327=1&GB328=0|good|auto|Conjunction of W01, W04-W09 in Grambank. Shows how rare the full consistent bundle is, even though each part is common.
N01|Optional plural word|r-5-2-6|la, optional with all nouns|GB:GB318=1 ; WALS:33A=7 ; WALS:34A=4|good|auto|33A = plural by a separate word; 34A = plural always optional for all nouns.
N02|No articles|r-5-2-7|definiteness from context or yin/fin/kin|GB:GB020=0&GB021=0 ; WALS:37A=5|good|auto|The two databases disagree: WALS 37A counts a demonstrative regularly used as an article (37A-2) as an article and gives common.
N03|Two-way demonstrative contrast|r-5-2-8;r-10-3-3|yin near, fin far|WALS:41A=2 ; GB:GB035=0|good|auto|GB035=0 = fewer than three distance contrasts.
N04|No numeral classifiers|r-5-2-5|noun + number|GB:GB057=0 ; WALS:55A=1|good|auto|
N05|No dual|r-10-3-7|singular and plural only|GB:GB031=0|good|auto|
N06|Inclusive/exclusive 'we'|r-10-3-7|kan incl., ta excl.|GB:GB028=1 ; WALS:39A=5|good|auto|
N07|Plural pronouns = person word + plural word|r-10-3-7|mon la, ba la (but kan/ta are own stems)|WALS:35A=8|fair|auto|Mixed in Apupiwe: 'we' has its own stems, 'you all' and 'they' add la.
N08|Decimal numerals|r-9-1-1;r-9-2-1|base 10, place words|WALS:131A=1 ; GB:GB333=1|good|auto|131A "decimal" excludes hybrid systems; GB333 counts any decimal system.
N09|Ordinals and labels from the cardinal with one marker|r-5-2-5|noun + mena + number for both "the third" and "document 2", including 'first' (mena ni)|WALS:53A=4|fair|auto|53A "one-th, two-th, three-th" = all ordinals regularly from cardinals including 'one'. Using the same marker for labels has no database feature; recalled, unverified: Mandarin di- + number does both (di san 'third', di san zhang 'chapter 3').
N10|Distributive 'each' distinct from 'all'|r-5-2-8|mesun vs we|GB:GB204=1|good|auto|
N11|No alienable/inalienable possession split|r-5-2-9|one pi for all|GB:GB059=0|good|auto|
N12|Indefinites built on generic nouns|r-5-2-8|ewo kin "someone" (person some), yefi kin "something"|WALS:46A=2|good|auto|
N13|Comparative with 'from' (separative) standard and a free 'more'|r-9-3-4|ke tugu don X|WALS:121A=1 ; GB:GB266=1 ; GB:GB276=1|good|auto|121A "locational" includes separative 'from'.
N14|'And' differs from 'with'|r-4-4-5|nun vs nan|GB:GB027=1 ; WALS:63A=1|good|auto|
N15|Same 'and' for nouns and verbs/clauses|r-4-4-5|nun|WALS:64A=1|good|auto|
N16|Endearment/diminutive marker on nouns and names|r-5-2-12|wi "dear little", a free word after the noun|GB:GB187=1|fair|auto|Grambank counts productive diminutive marking (mostly affixes); Apupiwe's is a free particle with affection as its core meaning.
N17|Modifier of a modifier marker|r-5-2-11|tan (MODMOD): lipo wiwi tan buda "dark-red cloth"|none|none|invented|No database feature and no natural precedent found (one web search: it found attributive linkers that join a modifier to a noun, such as Mandarin de and Burmese té, not a marker for a modifier of a modifier; inferred). Natural languages juxtapose or compound ('dark red', 'Dunkelrot').
N18|Relative clause by gap, marker gi, relation word stranded|r-5-3-2;r-5-3-4;r-5-3-6|ewo gi tedo; fela gi wan nume no ("place I live at")|WALS:122A=4 ; WALS:123A=4 ; WALS:123A=3|fair|auto|Subjects by gap (122A) is a good match. For obliques Apupiwe leaves the relation word behind at the clause end (gap) or keeps a pronoun after it (pronoun retention, 123A=3, the precise form). recalled, unverified: preposition stranding itself is rare outside Germanic.
N19|Relative-clause closer kiyu (also closes names, topics, derived words)|r-5-3-5;r-11-1;r-4-3-2;r-5-4-4|optional word closing every open relative clause|none|none|regional|sourced: Ewe "RCs begin with a relativizer and end with a clause-final marker"; Nkami "employs a bracket strategy, marking RCs at both left and right edges" (Dumah 2025, Ghana J. of Linguistics 14.2). recalled, unverified: Tok Pisin ia...ia brackets (Sankoff & Brown 1976). No world count found. Apupiwe goes beyond: one closer for four kinds of phrase, closing all open ones at once.
N20|Pauses carry grammar (close phrases, mark openers)|r-4-6-1|comma = pause, required in set places|none|none|converged|recalled, unverified: prosodic phrasing that marks syntactic constituents is found in all described spoken languages. Apupiwe goes beyond by making particular pauses obligatory and grammatical (a pause closes all open relative clauses).
V01|Copula with predicate nouns obligatory|r-4-7-2|mon gun lenu|WALS:120A=1 ; GB:GB117=1|good|auto|
V02|Property words take a copula when predicated|r-4-7-1;r-4-2-2|guyi gun tugu (bare guyi tugu reads as "big house")|WALS:118A=2 ; WALS:118A=2,3 ; GB:GB068=0|fair|auto|Apupiwe has no adjective class (V20); a bare state is also allowed in fragments.
V03|Same copula for nouns and places|r-4-7-3|mon gun no guyi|WALS:119A=2|good|auto|
V04|'Have' for possession|r-4-7-7|wan ibe pasu|WALS:117A=5 ; GB:GB250=1|good|auto|117A codes the main strategy only; GB250 = can be expressed with 'have'.
V05|'Have' with no subject = existential "there is"|r-4-7-7|ibe pasu "there's food"|none|none|common|sourced: "transitive verbs of possession and have-ILP constructions are not rare in the languages of the world"; found in Central/Southern Europe, Atlantic (West Africa) and "a vast region of Mainland South East Asia"; "Out of the 75 Pidgin and Creole varieties represented in [APiCS] ... 41 have a have-ILP construction" (Creissels 2019, Italian J. of Linguistics 31(2)). No world share.
V06|Negation by a free preverbal particle|r-6-2-2|su before the predicate|WALS:112A=2 ; WALS:143A=1 ; GB:GB299=1|good|auto|
V07|Negative concord with 'none'|r-6-2-2|su ibe poka din = one negation|WALS:115A=1|good|auto|115A = predicate negation also present with negative indefinites.
V08|One negator for all predicate types|r-6-2-2;r-4-7-7|su gun, su ibe, su + verb|GB:GB140=1|good|auto|
V09|Prohibitive = command particle + normal negator|r-4-2-8;r-12-4|ti su kusa|GB:GB139=0 ; WALS:71A=1|good|auto|
V10|Commands by a particle, not verb morphology|r-4-2-8;r-7-1-1|ti instruct, lo request; bare verb casual|WALS:70A=5 ; GB:GB519=1|fair|auto|70A=5 = no morphological 2nd-person imperative. GB519 = mood can be marked by a free particle.
V11|No inflectional optative or hortative; wish and 'let's' by lo|r-7-1-1;r-12-4|lo nubige kuwi ba; lo kan ...|WALS:73A=2 ; WALS:72A=4|fair|auto|WALS only codes inflectional optatives/hortatives; particle wishes are not counted.
V12|Statement unmarked|r-1-1;r-7-1-2|no particle|none|none|converged|recalled, unverified: declaratives are the unmarked sentence type in most languages (Sadock & Zwicky 1985; König & Siemund 2007). WALS 116A shows 173 languages mark polar questions by intonation only, i.e. declaratives carry no marker there.
V13|Advice particle|r-7-1-1|fon: advice, decision stays with the listener|none|none|invented|No database feature and no dedicated advice particle found in the sources read (not specifically searched beyond the speech-act sources below; inferred). Natural languages use modals ('should') or the imperative.
V14|Commissive (promissive) particle|r-7-1-1;r-7-1-4|du: the speaker commits|none|none|regional|sourced: Korean promissive called "this cross-linguistically rare type"; "Most languages express the illocutionary force of promising with a declarative" (Pak, Portner & Zanuttini 2008, Korean Linguistics 14).
V15|Warning particle|r-7-1-1|yo, yo yo emergency|none|none|common|sourced: apprehensional markers are "wide-spread cross-linguistically", described mostly in "Australian, Papuan, South Pacific, and Amazonian" languages (Vuillermet, Schultze-Berndt & Faller 2026, Language Science Press RCG 10). Fair match only: those mark feared events ('lest'); Apupiwe's yo is a warning speech act.
V16|Exclamation particle|r-7-1-1;r-7-1-3|ka|none|none|common|recalled, unverified: exclamatives are a recognised minor sentence type; dedicated particles exist in some languages (e.g. Mandarin a/ya). No count.
V17|Not-literal (irony) particle|r-7-1-1;r-7-1-3|genso, genso genso the whole turn|none|none|invented|sourced negative: no grammatical irony marker found. Closest: Cypriot-Greek siga, "a conventionalized ironic marker" that is lexical (Christodoulidou 2008, Pragmatics 18(2)); evidentials used ironically (Littell, Matthewson & Peterson 2010, UBCWPL 28).
V18|Polar question particle, clause-initial|r-4-8-1|bi ...|GB:GB262=1 ; WALS:92A=1 ; WALS:116A=1|good|auto|116A=1 = question particle anywhere.
V19|Tag at the end|r-4-8-1;r-7-2-2|..., bi|GB:GB263=1|fair|auto|GB263 = clause-final polar particle; a tag is a related but not identical thing. recalled, unverified: tag questions are near-universal.
V20|Content question words in situ|r-4-8-2|ba momu yu|GB:GB326=1 ; WALS:93A=2|good|auto|
V21|Question words built from one root|r-4-8-3|yu, ewo yu, no fela yu, bo yu ...|none|none|common|recalled, unverified: many languages build some interrogatives from a common element plus a generic noun (English wh-, Mandarin shenme + noun); fully regular sets are less common. No database feature.
V22|No yes/no words; answers echo the verb|r-4-8-6|obu / su obu|none|none|common|sourced: echo answers by "repeating the verbal predicate ... is the dominant echo answering strategy" (Fang 2025, Linguistic Typology 29(2), 26-language convenience sample). No world count. recalled, unverified: found in Chinese, Finnish, Welsh, Irish, Thai, Portuguese and others.
V23|Truth-based answer to negative questions|r-4-8-6|feko agrees with the negative|none|none|common|sourced: truth-based languages include Japanese, Korean, Mandarin, Thai, Lao, Yoruba, Amharic, Greek, Navajo, Malagasy; polarity-based include Swedish, Finnish, Irish, Turkish, Persian, Hungarian, Wolof, Basque (Holmberg, GLOW 36 handout, ~2013; not a balanced sample).
V24|Mirative stance word|r-7-2-5|donon "it turned out"|none|none|common|sourced: mirativity "may be expressed through a verbal affix, a complex predicate, or a pronoun"; "Evidentials ... may have mirative extensions"; "susceptible to linguistic diffusion" (Aikhenvald 2012, Linguistic Typology 16, abstract); "expressions of mirativity do not necessarily have an evidential component" (Hengeveld & Olbertz 2012). Status as a category disputed (Hill 2012). Dedicated particles, e.g. Cantonese wo: Wikipedia only (orientation).
V25|Shared-ground and softening stance words|r-7-2-3;r-7-2-4|yan "as you know", buni "okay?"|none|none|common|recalled, unverified: modal/discourse particles of this kind are common in particle-rich languages (German ja, Japanese ne/yo, Mandarin ma). No count.
V26|Politeness particle|r-10-2-1|wu, wu wu more|none|none|regional|No count found (searched). Nearest sourced: addressee-politeness verb marking (allocutivity) "is also found in a handful of other languages" beyond Basque (Wang, "Honorifics without [hon]", NLLT, 120-language survey). recalled, unverified: free politeness particles (Tagalog po, Thai khrap/kha) are mainly East and Southeast Asian.
V27|No polite pronouns (no T/V)|r-10-2-2|ba to anyone|GB:GB415=0 ; WALS:45A=1|good|auto|
V28|Gender-neutral respect title|r-10-1-3|lanno before name, role or kin word|none|none|common|recalled, unverified: honorific titles are widespread; gender-neutral ones (Japanese -san, Korean -ssi, Thai khun) are mostly East/Southeast Asian. No count.
V29|Address (vocative) at start or end|r-10-1-1|fo mara, ...|none|none|converged|recalled, unverified: every described language has some vocative/address form; position at clause edges is the norm.
V30|Obviation (topic vs non-topic third person)|r-10-3-1;r-10-3-2|mon topic / tun other person; bun topic / fin other thing|none|none|regional|sourced: "Obviation, a hallmark property of the Algonquian languages, is a typologically rare phenomenon" (Bliss 2017, WPLC 27(1)); "only Kutenai and Algonquian have obviation systems in this narrow sense" (Dryer, Algonquian Conference paper, 2006 ms). Apupiwe extends it to things.
V31|Logophoric pronoun|r-10-3-4|son = the person whose words are reported|GB:GB167=1|good|auto|recalled, unverified: concentrated in West and Central Africa (Hyman & Comrie 1981; Culy 1994).
V32|Generic human pronoun|r-10-3-5|dan "anyone, people in general"|none|none|regional|sourced: man-constructions are "a yet unnoticed areal feature of the Standard Average European area, but ... a recessive areal feature" (Giacalone Ramat & Sansò 2007, SLCS 88, abstract). Elsewhere the impersonal 3rd plural is found in "most macro-areas" (Siewierska, Ways of impersonalizing).
V33|Reflexive = intensifier 'self'|r-10-3-6|nen: kuwi nen; mon nen obu|WALS:47A=1 ; GB:GB305=1|good|auto|GB305 = independent reflexive pronoun.
V34|Reciprocal distinct from reflexive|r-1-5|nen nen "each other"|WALS:106A=2|fair|auto|Apupiwe's reciprocal is the doubled reflexive, a form related to it.
V35|Subject (and other) arguments may be dropped|r-4-5-1|anything recoverable|GB:GB522=1 ; WALS:101A=5|good|auto|101A=5 = optional subject pronouns (no affixes).
V36|Patient-labile verbs|r-4-5-4|beya ben tapi "the door closed"|GB:GB401=1|good|auto|
V37|Double-object ditransitive, recipient first|r-4-2-5|wiwa ba nuta|WALS:105A=2 ; GB:GB105=1|good|auto|
V38|Passive-like state of the undergoer, no agent phrase|r-4-7-4|gun tapi "is closed", mon gun kuwi|WALS:107A=1 ; GB:GB302=1|fair|auto|107A counts any passive; GB302 = free passive marker. Apupiwe's gun + verb is closer to a resultative; no agent can be added. Counted by GB302 (a free marker, as Apupiwe's is), it would be regional.
V39|No morphological causative; periphrastic 'make'|r-4-2-7|tula upe yin "fix this"|WALS:111A=1 ; GB:GB155=0|good|auto|
V40|Verb sequences (serial verbs)|r-4-2-6|doyu pume "sit thinking", isi yinmi "go through the forest"|GB:GB118=1|fair|auto|Apupiwe's verb + verb covers both serialisation and complements.
V41|'Want' with the shared subject left out|r-4-2-6|momu isi|WALS:124A=1|good|auto|
V42|Aspect by free preverbal particles|r-8-1-2|ben done, li ongoing, buntu about to|GB:GB520=1|good|auto|
V43|No tense; time words set the frame|r-8-1-1;r-8-1-2|ben reads as past only with no frame|WALS:66A=4 ; WALS:67A=2 ; GB:GB083=0&GB084=0&GB521=0|fair|auto|ben defaults to past, so Apupiwe is close to languages with a completive that implies past.
V44|Habitual marker|r-8-1-4|manni "usually"; with past frame "used to"|none|none|common|recalled, unverified: habitual grams are among the commonest aspect categories (Dahl 1985; Bybee, Perkins & Pagliuca 1994). No count read.
V45|'Not yet' = 'still' + 'not'|r-8-1-3|li su|none|none|common|sourced: "the most widespread type of continuative expressions is represented by ... monosemous emphatic continuative adverbials which in combination with negation acquire a 'not yet' meaning" (Panova 2023, Linguistic Typology at the Crossroads; 120-language stratified sample). Veselinova (2015 slides, 81 languages): 28 grammaticalised not-yet markers, 44 phasal adverbial or none.
V46|Modal verbs separate from epistemic marking|r-12-2;r-6-1-4|nebi, kifa, wupi, efon; "must = probably" goes to pe|WALS:76A=3 ; WALS:74A=2|good|auto|76A=3 = no overlap between situational and epistemic modal marking.
V47|Evidentials: optional, three-way (seen, told, inferred)|r-6-1-3|wanme / inwun / wemen, free clause-initial words|GB:GB322=1&GB323=1 ; WALS:77A=3 ; WALS:78A=4|good|auto|sourced: "Only about a quarter of the languages of the world have grammatical evidentials"; three-term type "B1. Direct (or Visual), Inferred, Reported" (Aikhenvald 2004, Evidentiality, front matter). Apupiwe's is optional; Aikhenvald has sections on "Omission of evidentials" (not read), so how common optional systems are is not sourced here.
V48|Graded confidence: pe + sixths (and percent)|r-6-1-2;r-6-1-4|pe mu ... pe ufo, pe kin "maybe"|WALS:75A=1,2,3|poor|invented|Every WALS 75A language has some epistemic-possibility marker (that part is near universal). A numeric scale of grammatical confidence has no natural precedent found (one search: nothing; inferred).
V49|Counterfactual as zero confidence in an if-clause|r-12-1|sin pe mu ...|none|none|invented|The distinction is common (recalled, unverified: most languages can mark counterfactual conditionals, often with "fake past", Iatridou 2000); doing it with a probability value is Apupiwe's own (follows from V48).
V50|Topic marker 'as for X' at the start|r-4-3-2|pi + noun phrase, pause|none|none|common|sourced: topic particles are well studied for Korean and Japanese "but they are widespread in many other Asian languages (Boro 2021) and cross-linguistically (Wälchli 2022)" (Cimmino & Ozerov 2025, Linguistic Typology at the Crossroads 5-1). No world count. Reusing 'of' as the topic marker is Apupiwe's own.
V51|Focus and additive particles|r-6-2-3|min focus, ga only, pon also, yon even|none|none|converged|recalled, unverified: 'only', 'also', 'even' words are found in all well-described languages (König 1991, The meaning of focus particles). A dedicated contrastive-focus particle (min) is less general; not counted.
V52|Doubling to intensify, any word|r-1-5|su su, yo yo, wu wu, feko feko|GB:GB160=1 ; WALS:27A=1,2|fair|auto|GB160 = elements other than nouns and verbs reduplicated. Apupiwe gives fixed meanings to doubled grammar words, which goes beyond.
V53|Derivation markers for doer, thing, tool, place|r-5-4-2|win / gen / to / sen before a verb|GB:GB048=1 ; GB:GB049=1 ; GB:GB047=1|fair|auto|Grambank codes agent, object and action nominalisers only (any form, mostly affixes); instrument and place have no database feature.
V54|Class-free roots: any content word in any role by position|r-4-2-3|mosu "apologise / apology"; state via gun, event as verb, description via gi|none|none|regional|sourced: in Hengeveld, Rijkhoff & Siewierska's 50-language sample about 11 are flexible (types 1-3), but only Samoan and Tagalog (and Mundari, 1/2) are fully flexible type 1 (J. of Linguistics 40, 2004); contested: "Mundari does have clearly definable word classes" (Evans & Osada 2005, Linguistic Typology 9). Apupiwe is type 1.
V55|Name marker|r-11-1|fo + name; closes at grammar word or kiyu|none|none|regional|sourced: "The Austronesian macrophylum provides ample evidence for the distinction of common articles ... and [proprial articles]"; "there is as yet no generally accepted unitary definition" (Stolz & Levkovych, DetMod abstract). No world count. Elsewhere: Catalan en/na, Fijian, Greek article with names (recalled).
V56|Quote of several sentences bracketed by the same word|r-4-4-6|kenlin ... kenlin; one-sentence quote after a say-verb|none|none|invented|Quotative markers are common (recalled, unverified: Güldemann 2008; source blocked). No language found that opens and closes a quote with the same marker (searched; not found). The one-sentence say-verb frame is the ordinary direct-speech pattern.
